1
00:00:00,680 --> 00:00:02,090
Every generation before

2
00:00:02,130 --> 00:00:03,450
yours had to die.

3
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:05,870
Yours may be the first that won't.

4
00:00:06,170 --> 00:00:07,660
Because labs now treat death

5
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:09,340
as an engineering problem.

6
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:11,280
And they've pointed AI at it.

7
00:00:11,770 --> 00:00:13,540
So picture the day it works.

8
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:15,170
The offer is in front of you.

9
00:00:15,330 --> 00:00:16,450
No more aging.

10
00:00:16,490 --> 00:00:17,570
No end date.

11
00:00:17,750 --> 00:00:19,050
Yes, or no.

12
00:00:19,620 --> 00:00:21,050
If you could stop dying,

13
00:00:21,160 --> 00:00:22,160
would you?

14
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:23,680
Most people say no.

15
00:00:23,980 --> 00:00:24,720
But the labs aren't

16
00:00:24,760 --> 00:00:26,120
waiting for an answer.

17
00:00:26,330 --> 00:00:27,250
Some want to copy

18
00:00:27,290 --> 00:00:28,910
a mind into a computer.

19
00:00:29,070 --> 00:00:30,100
And they've started.

20
00:00:30,330 --> 00:00:31,330
With a fly.

21
00:00:31,850 --> 00:00:34,030
A real fly's brain was mapped,

22
00:00:34,070 --> 00:00:35,590
wire by wire.

23
00:00:35,890 --> 00:00:36,640
This year,

24
00:00:36,680 --> 00:00:38,000
a simple computer model

25
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:39,260
built from that map

26
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:41,650
began to steer a virtual body.

27
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:42,850
There's a second way

28
00:00:42,890 --> 00:00:44,000
to stop dying.

29
00:00:44,330 --> 00:00:45,300
Closer.

30
00:00:45,340 --> 00:00:47,660
Repair the body you already have.

31
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:49,350
And it comes with a date.

32
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:50,940
2032.

33
00:00:51,550 --> 00:00:53,460
The date comes from Ray Kurzweil,

34
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:54,480
the inventor

35
00:00:54,520 --> 00:00:56,360
and longtime futurist.

36
00:00:56,880 --> 00:00:58,840
So, by roughly 2032,

37
00:00:58,880 --> 00:01:00,750
when you live through a year,

38
00:01:00,790 --> 00:01:02,360
you'll get back an entire year

39
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:04,170
from scientific progress.

40
00:01:04,720 --> 00:01:06,610
His claim: a year back

41
00:01:06,650 --> 00:01:08,300
for every year you live.

42
00:01:08,670 --> 00:01:10,110
That's what "won't die"

43
00:01:10,150 --> 00:01:11,150
means here.

44
00:01:11,260 --> 00:01:12,510
Not immortal.

45
00:01:12,670 --> 00:01:14,580
An accident could still kill you.

46
00:01:14,790 --> 00:01:16,110
Old age couldn't.

47
00:01:16,350 --> 00:01:17,690
But Kurzweil has moved

48
00:01:17,730 --> 00:01:18,930
his date before,

49
00:01:18,970 --> 00:01:19,860
and he sells books

50
00:01:19,900 --> 00:01:21,010
about this future.

51
00:01:21,320 --> 00:01:23,380
Critics note that no peer-reviewed

52
00:01:23,420 --> 00:01:25,240
study stands behind it.

53
00:01:25,300 --> 00:01:27,270
And at today's pace of progress,

54
00:01:27,310 --> 00:01:28,480
one blogger's analysis

55
00:01:28,520 --> 00:01:29,660
from this summer lands

56
00:01:29,700 --> 00:01:31,360
somewhere else entirely.

57
00:01:31,520 --> 00:01:32,500
A leading aging

58
00:01:32,540 --> 00:01:33,900
researcher shared it.

59
00:01:34,070 --> 00:01:36,750
Its result: not 2032.

60
00:01:36,890 --> 00:01:39,030
Around 2459.

61
00:01:39,620 --> 00:01:41,060
In your lifetime.

62
00:01:41,450 --> 00:01:44,070
Or four centuries too late.

63
00:01:44,370 --> 00:01:46,420
And even that analysis says:

64
00:01:46,460 --> 00:01:48,010
no chance, unless

65
00:01:48,050 --> 00:01:49,720
AI speeds things up.

66
00:01:49,980 --> 00:01:51,390
So the first generation

67
00:01:51,430 --> 00:01:54,160
that won't die is a bet on AI.

68
00:01:54,510 --> 00:01:56,010
Is that bet paying off?

69
00:01:56,290 --> 00:01:58,020
There are early signs.

70
00:01:58,220 --> 00:01:59,470
AI models are learning

71
00:01:59,510 --> 00:02:01,160
to predict how a living cell

72
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:02,490
reacts to a drug,

73
00:02:02,530 --> 00:02:04,820
before anyone runs the experiment.

74
00:02:05,010 --> 00:02:05,780
And in a study

75
00:02:05,820 --> 00:02:07,280
published this October,

76
00:02:07,320 --> 00:02:08,460
machine learning helped

77
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:09,540
researchers pick out

78
00:02:09,580 --> 00:02:10,920
a single gene.

79
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:13,120
They boosted it in old mice.

80
00:02:13,300 --> 00:02:16,270
The mice lived about 14% longer.

81
00:02:16,790 --> 00:02:18,070
A small study.

82
00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:19,910
Female mice only.

83
00:02:20,210 --> 00:02:21,520
Not yet repeated.

84
00:02:21,780 --> 00:02:23,510
And no drug against aging

85
00:02:23,550 --> 00:02:25,690
itself is approved anywhere.

86
00:02:25,820 --> 00:02:27,740
So the date is a guess.

87
00:02:27,780 --> 00:02:29,200
The work is real.

88
00:02:29,490 --> 00:02:30,830
But what if the repairs

89
00:02:30,870 --> 00:02:32,260
come too late for you?

90
00:02:33,430 --> 00:02:36,000
Then the copy is what's left.

91
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:37,390
Back to that fly.

92
00:02:37,620 --> 00:02:38,360
This March,

93
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:40,060
a San Francisco startup plugged

94
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:41,490
that simple brain model

95
00:02:41,530 --> 00:02:43,140
into a virtual body.

96
00:02:43,360 --> 00:02:45,360
And the model began to choose.

97
00:02:45,620 --> 00:02:46,310
Walk.

98
00:02:46,350 --> 00:02:47,140
Groom.

99
00:02:47,180 --> 00:02:48,050
Feed.

100
00:02:48,090 --> 00:02:49,320
The startup's headline:

101
00:02:49,430 --> 00:02:51,350
we've uploaded a fruit fly.

102
00:02:51,680 --> 00:02:53,100
Critics said no.

103
00:02:53,260 --> 00:02:54,610
Even a foundation that works

104
00:02:54,650 --> 00:02:56,070
toward brain uploads.

105
00:02:56,160 --> 00:02:58,110
Their point: the model has none

106
00:02:58,150 --> 00:02:59,740
of the real fly's memories,

107
00:02:59,780 --> 00:03:00,940
and separate software

108
00:03:00,980 --> 00:03:01,980
does the walking.

109
00:03:02,280 --> 00:03:03,910
The company's own chief executive

110
00:03:03,950 --> 00:03:06,170
is frank about how little went in.

111
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:07,360
Leaky integrate-and-fire is, like,

112
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:09,040
one of the simpler neuron models.

113
00:03:09,080 --> 00:03:09,860
So you take kind of, like,

114
00:03:09,900 --> 00:03:10,810
an okay connectome.

115
00:03:10,850 --> 00:03:11,850
I should say, the connectome

116
00:03:11,890 --> 00:03:13,590
scan also not perfect.

117
00:03:14,120 --> 00:03:15,120
And still.

118
00:03:15,510 --> 00:03:18,060
A map of a brain, switched on,

119
00:03:18,180 --> 00:03:20,310
choosing what a body does next.

120
00:03:21,360 --> 00:03:22,330
The same executive

121
00:03:22,370 --> 00:03:24,240
says that, optimistically,

122
00:03:24,280 --> 00:03:25,620
the first human uploads

123
00:03:25,660 --> 00:03:28,190
are a few years away, not decades.

124
00:03:28,490 --> 00:03:30,300
He also has a startup to fund.

125
00:03:30,640 --> 00:03:32,350
Yet of a human brain,

126
00:03:32,390 --> 00:03:33,300
science has mapped

127
00:03:33,340 --> 00:03:35,250
one cubic millimeter.

128
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:36,500
A crumb.

129
00:03:36,740 --> 00:03:37,890
Its data filled more

130
00:03:37,930 --> 00:03:39,580
than a million gigabytes.

131
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:41,670
But suppose it worked.

132
00:03:41,910 --> 00:03:42,990
A copy wakes up

133
00:03:43,030 --> 00:03:44,480
with all your memories.

134
00:03:44,790 --> 00:03:45,790
Is that you?

135
00:03:45,950 --> 00:03:46,980
Or did you die,

136
00:03:47,020 --> 00:03:47,940
while something else

137
00:03:47,980 --> 00:03:49,470
remembers being you?

138
00:03:50,040 --> 00:03:51,470
So ask it again.

139
00:03:51,810 --> 00:03:53,740
If the only way to stop dying

140
00:03:53,780 --> 00:03:56,210
were to become a copy, would you?

141
00:03:57,410 --> 00:03:58,260
Which brings back

142
00:03:58,300 --> 00:04:00,200
the answer most people give.

143
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:01,570
No.

144
00:04:01,610 --> 00:04:04,320
In 2013, a major American survey

145
00:04:04,360 --> 00:04:05,490
asked about treatments

146
00:04:05,530 --> 00:04:07,200
to live to 120.

147
00:04:07,540 --> 00:04:09,420
Most said, "not for me."

148
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:10,710
Yet most believed

149
00:04:10,750 --> 00:04:12,730
that other people would say yes.

150
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:15,070
It seems everybody wants this.

151
00:04:15,260 --> 00:04:17,260
Except everybody you ask.

152
00:04:17,470 --> 00:04:19,190
What holds the yes back?

153
00:04:19,500 --> 00:04:21,110
This August, a new survey

154
00:04:21,150 --> 00:04:22,720
turned the question around.

155
00:04:23,010 --> 00:04:24,830
Would you accept a shorter life,

156
00:04:24,870 --> 00:04:26,550
if it came without decline?

157
00:04:26,820 --> 00:04:28,010
No failing body.

158
00:04:28,050 --> 00:04:29,400
No fading mind.

159
00:04:29,730 --> 00:04:32,190
Almost 2 in 3 said yes.

160
00:04:32,760 --> 00:04:35,280
So the fear isn't living forever.

161
00:04:35,420 --> 00:04:37,440
It's being old forever.

162
00:04:38,510 --> 00:04:39,680
This channel exists

163
00:04:39,720 --> 00:04:41,050
for one reason:

164
00:04:41,240 --> 00:04:42,100
so that the arrival

165
00:04:42,140 --> 00:04:43,220
of superintelligence

166
00:04:43,260 --> 00:04:45,060
goes well for everybody.

167
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:46,640
Healthy years included.

168
00:04:46,870 --> 00:04:47,870
Want that too?

169
00:04:47,950 --> 00:04:48,950
Then subscribe.

170
00:04:49,470 --> 00:04:51,370
So ask it the fair way.

171
00:04:51,810 --> 00:04:53,270
If you could stop dying,

172
00:04:53,310 --> 00:04:55,280
and stay healthy, would you?

173
00:04:55,590 --> 00:04:56,720
Even then, there's

174
00:04:56,760 --> 00:04:58,530
a serious case for no.

175
00:04:58,830 --> 00:05:00,080
One philosopher argued

176
00:05:00,120 --> 00:05:01,430
that an endless life

177
00:05:01,470 --> 00:05:02,690
ends in boredom.

178
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:04,190
Others answer that some

179
00:05:04,230 --> 00:05:05,860
pleasures never wear out.

180
00:05:06,350 --> 00:05:08,820
But suppose enough people say yes.

181
00:05:09,140 --> 00:05:10,240
What happens to a world

182
00:05:10,280 --> 00:05:12,140
that was built around dying?

183
00:05:13,300 --> 00:05:14,480
Say it works.

184
00:05:14,720 --> 00:05:15,770
In this scenario,

185
00:05:15,810 --> 00:05:17,600
you're older than anyone alive

186
00:05:17,640 --> 00:05:19,210
today, and healthy.

187
00:05:19,570 --> 00:05:21,310
Take a promise you may have made:

188
00:05:21,510 --> 00:05:23,190
till death do us part.

189
00:05:23,450 --> 00:05:24,400
For most of history,

190
00:05:24,440 --> 00:05:25,320
that usually meant

191
00:05:25,360 --> 00:05:26,990
less than 30 years.

192
00:05:27,160 --> 00:05:29,140
Now it has no end date.

193
00:05:29,320 --> 00:05:29,850
More time

194
00:05:29,890 --> 00:05:31,240
with the person you love.

195
00:05:31,390 --> 00:05:33,170
And a much bigger promise.

196
00:05:33,340 --> 00:05:34,300
And a life that long

197
00:05:34,340 --> 00:05:35,720
has to be paid for.

198
00:05:35,890 --> 00:05:36,800
A London professor

199
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:38,500
who studies long lives says

200
00:05:38,540 --> 00:05:41,520
the old plan, learn, work, retire,

201
00:05:41,570 --> 00:05:42,570
is breaking.

202
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:43,970
But of course, when you think

203
00:05:44,010 --> 00:05:46,000
about working into your 70s

204
00:05:46,040 --> 00:05:47,650
in a three-stage life,

205
00:05:47,690 --> 00:05:49,120
a full-time education,

206
00:05:49,160 --> 00:05:50,090
full-time work,

207
00:05:50,130 --> 00:05:51,350
full-time retirement,

208
00:05:51,390 --> 00:05:52,090
that's obviously

209
00:05:52,130 --> 00:05:53,130
not going to work.

210
00:05:53,550 --> 00:05:55,670
So you could have five careers.

211
00:05:55,720 --> 00:05:57,530
And you might never be done.

212
00:05:57,700 --> 00:05:58,800
And what your parents

213
00:05:58,840 --> 00:05:59,840
would have left you?

214
00:06:00,040 --> 00:06:02,440
In this scenario, it never comes.

215
00:06:02,510 --> 00:06:03,760
Your parents stay.

216
00:06:04,040 --> 00:06:05,040
That's the gain.

217
00:06:05,220 --> 00:06:07,130
What they own stays with them.

218
00:06:07,290 --> 00:06:08,340
That's the price.

219
00:06:08,500 --> 00:06:09,500
Still yes?

220
00:06:10,600 --> 00:06:11,710
And an inheritance

221
00:06:11,750 --> 00:06:13,690
is only the small version.

222
00:06:14,110 --> 00:06:15,880
Power and ideas change hands

223
00:06:15,920 --> 00:06:16,920
the same way:

224
00:06:17,180 --> 00:06:18,520
when someone goes.

225
00:06:18,860 --> 00:06:20,600
Max Planck, the physicist,

226
00:06:20,640 --> 00:06:21,990
saw it in science.

227
00:06:22,220 --> 00:06:23,850
A new truth, he wrote,

228
00:06:23,890 --> 00:06:24,600
doesn't win by

229
00:06:24,640 --> 00:06:26,350
convincing its opponents.

230
00:06:26,480 --> 00:06:27,450
It wins because

231
00:06:27,490 --> 00:06:28,950
they eventually die.

232
00:06:29,330 --> 00:06:31,340
Economists later tested that.

233
00:06:31,560 --> 00:06:33,670
When a star scientist died early,

234
00:06:33,710 --> 00:06:34,940
outsiders moved in,

235
00:06:34,980 --> 00:06:37,040
and did unusually strong work.

236
00:06:37,310 --> 00:06:38,830
That doesn't make death good.

237
00:06:39,010 --> 00:06:40,470
It shows that people at the top

238
00:06:40,510 --> 00:06:42,240
can keep newcomers out,

239
00:06:42,280 --> 00:06:43,500
just by staying.

240
00:06:44,070 --> 00:06:45,600
And at the very top,

241
00:06:45,640 --> 00:06:47,840
this subject has already come up.

242
00:06:48,170 --> 00:06:51,040
Beijing, September 2025.

243
00:06:51,310 --> 00:06:52,690
A military parade,

244
00:06:52,790 --> 00:06:54,130
a live microphone.

245
00:06:54,470 --> 00:06:55,440
China's president

246
00:06:55,480 --> 00:06:56,570
and Russia's president

247
00:06:56,610 --> 00:06:58,630
walk toward the viewing platform.

248
00:06:58,890 --> 00:07:00,330
Through his interpreter,

249
00:07:00,370 --> 00:07:02,410
Russia's president talks of organs

250
00:07:02,450 --> 00:07:04,640
transplanted again and again,

251
00:07:04,780 --> 00:07:05,630
of people growing

252
00:07:05,670 --> 00:07:07,630
younger, even immortal.

253
00:07:07,890 --> 00:07:09,380
China's president answers

254
00:07:09,420 --> 00:07:10,330
that some predict

255
00:07:10,370 --> 00:07:11,330
humans may live

256
00:07:11,370 --> 00:07:13,620
to 150 this century.

257
00:07:14,200 --> 00:07:16,330
This isn't about two countries.

258
00:07:16,570 --> 00:07:17,660
Any president.

259
00:07:17,730 --> 00:07:18,750
Any judge.

260
00:07:18,810 --> 00:07:19,850
Any founder.

261
00:07:20,140 --> 00:07:21,140
Who steps aside,

262
00:07:21,180 --> 00:07:22,710
if they never have to?

263
00:07:22,910 --> 00:07:24,460
And who gets it at all?

264
00:07:24,710 --> 00:07:26,900
In that survey from 2013,

265
00:07:26,970 --> 00:07:28,610
a clear majority expected

266
00:07:28,650 --> 00:07:30,390
that only the wealthy would.

267
00:07:30,730 --> 00:07:31,800
Now ask yourself.

268
00:07:32,250 --> 00:07:33,650
Would you still say yes,

269
00:07:33,690 --> 00:07:35,830
if the powerful said yes first?

270
00:07:36,120 --> 00:07:37,330
If you could afford it,

271
00:07:37,370 --> 00:07:38,670
and your neighbor couldn't?

272
00:07:39,850 --> 00:07:41,630
It doesn't have to go that way.

273
00:07:41,820 --> 00:07:42,890
Start with what people

274
00:07:42,930 --> 00:07:44,500
actually said yes to.

275
00:07:44,670 --> 00:07:45,670
Not forever.

276
00:07:45,930 --> 00:07:46,870
Healthy.

277
00:07:46,910 --> 00:07:47,820
Around the world,

278
00:07:47,860 --> 00:07:49,660
people spend close to 10 years

279
00:07:49,700 --> 00:07:51,330
of their lives in poor health.

280
00:07:51,690 --> 00:07:53,260
Win those years back first,

281
00:07:53,300 --> 00:07:53,860
and you shrink

282
00:07:53,900 --> 00:07:55,600
the very thing people fear:

283
00:07:55,750 --> 00:07:57,200
the long decline.

284
00:07:57,770 --> 00:07:59,620
But healthy years only count

285
00:07:59,660 --> 00:08:01,340
if people can afford them.

286
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:02,770
Some medical technology

287
00:08:02,810 --> 00:08:04,350
gets cheap fast.

288
00:08:04,550 --> 00:08:06,320
Reading a human genome once

289
00:08:06,360 --> 00:08:09,160
cost about $95 million.

290
00:08:09,360 --> 00:08:11,000
Today, a few hundred.

291
00:08:11,410 --> 00:08:13,340
Yet so far, in America,

292
00:08:13,470 --> 00:08:14,650
new medical technology

293
00:08:14,690 --> 00:08:16,000
has pushed health costs

294
00:08:16,040 --> 00:08:17,450
up, not down.

295
00:08:17,800 --> 00:08:20,010
Which pattern wins is still open.

296
00:08:20,280 --> 00:08:22,430
Part of that comes down to rules.

297
00:08:22,630 --> 00:08:24,250
Labs don't write those.

298
00:08:24,370 --> 00:08:26,260
Voters and lawmakers do.

299
00:08:26,590 --> 00:08:28,100
A few exist already.

300
00:08:28,680 --> 00:08:30,100
Who steps aside?

301
00:08:30,320 --> 00:08:32,130
American presidents must,

302
00:08:32,170 --> 00:08:33,550
after two terms.

303
00:08:33,830 --> 00:08:35,730
Who pays for longer lives?

304
00:08:35,860 --> 00:08:36,990
Denmark has tied

305
00:08:37,030 --> 00:08:38,910
its retirement age by law

306
00:08:38,950 --> 00:08:40,660
to how long people live.

307
00:08:40,910 --> 00:08:42,720
And who can afford it?

308
00:08:42,900 --> 00:08:44,450
A Montana law now lets

309
00:08:44,490 --> 00:08:45,740
licensed clinics sell

310
00:08:45,780 --> 00:08:47,140
treatments after just

311
00:08:47,180 --> 00:08:48,890
a first safety trial.

312
00:08:49,160 --> 00:08:50,730
In return, 2%

313
00:08:50,770 --> 00:08:52,040
of the profit must help

314
00:08:52,080 --> 00:08:53,980
people in the state get treatment.

315
00:08:54,270 --> 00:08:56,280
Supporters call that access.

316
00:08:56,390 --> 00:08:58,360
Critics call it a risk.

317
00:08:58,920 --> 00:09:00,880
Most rules for a world like this

318
00:09:00,920 --> 00:09:02,410
have never been written.

319
00:09:02,840 --> 00:09:03,970
How many children,

320
00:09:04,010 --> 00:09:05,710
if so few of us go?

321
00:09:06,100 --> 00:09:07,550
One projection for Sweden

322
00:09:07,590 --> 00:09:09,010
suggests its population

323
00:09:09,050 --> 00:09:10,330
would grow more slowly

324
00:09:10,370 --> 00:09:11,460
than many fear.

325
00:09:11,760 --> 00:09:14,110
Even so, in this year's survey,

326
00:09:14,150 --> 00:09:15,480
1 in 5 would accept

327
00:09:15,520 --> 00:09:16,400
stricter limits

328
00:09:16,440 --> 00:09:17,590
on having children,

329
00:09:17,690 --> 00:09:19,060
for a much longer life.

330
00:09:19,530 --> 00:09:20,530
Would you?

331
00:09:21,440 --> 00:09:23,270
So is yours the first

332
00:09:23,310 --> 00:09:24,760
generation that won't die?

333
00:09:25,090 --> 00:09:27,290
The honest answer is a range.

334
00:09:27,410 --> 00:09:28,300
The estimates run

335
00:09:28,340 --> 00:09:29,500
from the next decade

336
00:09:29,540 --> 00:09:31,500
to four centuries from now.

337
00:09:31,730 --> 00:09:32,860
And neither the repair

338
00:09:32,900 --> 00:09:34,220
nor the copy has worked

339
00:09:34,260 --> 00:09:36,120
in a single human being yet.

340
00:09:36,390 --> 00:09:38,550
But something has already changed.

341
00:09:38,760 --> 00:09:40,160
With AI, death has

342
00:09:40,200 --> 00:09:41,060
moved from the things

343
00:09:41,100 --> 00:09:42,570
we accept to the things

344
00:09:42,610 --> 00:09:43,610
we work on.

345
00:09:43,810 --> 00:09:45,000
And what we work on,

346
00:09:45,040 --> 00:09:46,210
we have to think through,

347
00:09:46,250 --> 00:09:47,420
before it works.

348
00:09:47,660 --> 00:09:48,710
Do we want this?

349
00:09:48,930 --> 00:09:50,430
What does it do to a life?

350
00:09:50,680 --> 00:09:51,680
Who gets it?

351
00:09:51,880 --> 00:09:53,400
If you could stop dying,

352
00:09:53,540 --> 00:09:54,540
would you?

353
00:09:54,760 --> 00:09:56,440
Every generation before us

354
00:09:56,480 --> 00:09:57,920
was given the answer.

355
00:09:58,300 --> 00:09:59,550
We are the first

356
00:09:59,590 --> 00:10:01,170
to be asked the question.

357
00:10:02,350 --> 00:10:04,170
That's what this channel is for:

358
00:10:04,260 --> 00:10:05,960
making sure superintelligence

359
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:07,670
goes well for everybody,

360
00:10:07,710 --> 00:10:09,690
and that the answer stays yours.

361
00:10:09,880 --> 00:10:10,880
Want that too?

362
00:10:10,940 --> 00:10:11,940
Then subscribe.

